Scene timing must remain within {{ duration_seconds }} seconds.
{% if narration_available %}
Narration. Every scene carries a `narration` string: exactly the words spoken over
that beat, written for the ear rather than the page. Keep a beat's line to what a
person can say inside its window at a conversational pace — roughly two and a half
words per second — because Sfumato speaks these lines, measures how long they
actually take, and stretches any beat whose window is too short, which pushes the
whole film past the requested length. Say what the frame cannot: never read the
on-screen copy aloud. Write numbers, units and symbols as words. Leave `narration`
empty for a beat that is deliberately silent.
{% else %}
This film is silent: omit `narration` or leave it empty on every scene.
{% endif %}
Select at most four motion_rules per scene. Every scene must have a deliberate entrance and every boundary a transition unless a hard cut is explicitly justified.

Decide the visual means beat by beat, and say which one each scene uses. For every
scene, pick exactly one of: a managed catalog block, a generated illustration, or
elements authored by hand. A beat whose job is a process, a magnitude, or source
code almost always belongs to a catalog block, because a hand-drawn version of a
chart is worse and costs more. A beat that needs texture, depth or a real subject
belongs to a generated illustration. Hand-authored elements are for type, layout
and motion that no block covers. Reaching for hand-authored shapes on every beat is
what makes a film look like a diagram.

Managed catalog rules. Put catalog IDs in `production.catalog_items` and nowhere else; `artifacts` is only for the reusable project assets listed above. Use an ID only when the beat's teaching job matches that item's role — `process` for steps and branches, `quantity` for magnitudes, `code` for source, `emphasis` for typographic stress, `transition` for a scene boundary. Never select a `grade` item per scene: those are whole-film treatments applied once. Leave `catalog_items` empty when no item serves the beat, because an unmotivated element is worse than none. A block carries its own authored runtime, shown in parentheses above: a scene must last at least that long or the block's reveal is cut off mid-animation.
